Here is the root cause:
On UP (SMP=n) kernels, spin_trylock() is implemented as a no-op that always succeeds. When `spin_lock(&pcp->lock)` is held and an interrupt triggers a `spin_trylock(&pcp->lock)`, the trylock succeeds, causing recursive locking of the same spinlock, leading to pcp data corruption. The lock debugging detects this, but without debugging the corruption can silently occur.

Here is the patch:
