The code statements that lead to the bug are:
spin_lock(&pcp->lock);
free_pcppages_bulk(zone, to_drain, pcp, 0);
spin_unlock(&pcp->lock);

Reason:
On UP (SMP=n) kernels, spin_trylock() is a no-op and always succeeds. When an interrupt occurs while spin_lock(&pcp->lock) is held, the interrupt handler may call __free_frozen_pages() which attempts spin_trylock(&pcp->lock). The trylock succeeds, leading to recursive locking of the same spinlock and corruption of the pcp structure.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
#define pcp_spin_lock_maybe_irqsave(ptr, flags) spin_lock_irqsave(&(ptr)->lock, flags)
#define pcp_spin_unlock_maybe_irqrestore(ptr, flags) spin_unlock_irqrestore(&(ptr)->lock, flags)
pcp_spin_lock_maybe_irqsave(pcp, UP_flags);
free_pcppages_bulk(zone, to_drain, pcp, 0);
pcp_spin_unlock_maybe_irqrestore(pcp, UP_flags);

Reason:
On UP, the new macros replace spin_lock/spin_unlock with spin_lock_irqsave/spin_unlock_irqrestore, which disable interrupts while holding the lock. This prevents an interrupt from triggering a nested spin_trylock on the same lock, thus avoiding the recursive lock acquisition and pcp corruption. On SMP or PREEMPT_RT, the macros use the original spin_lock/spin_unlock since the trylock will correctly fail there.

